# Problem setup:

## Points

- A(0.4127, 0.5303)
- B(0.9202, 0.6639)
- C(0.5468, 1.2823)
- D(0.9882, 0.8778)
- H(0.6992, 0.7034)

## Assumptions:

[C0] : BH = BD
[C1] : AH ⟂ BC
[C2] : BC ⟂ DH
[C3] : AC ⟂ BH

## Numerical checks

[N0] : ▲BDH has the same orientation as ▲BDH
[N1] : A ≠ D
[N2] : A ≠ H
[N3] : D ≠ H
[N4] : B, C, D are not collinear


# Goals

ABCD are cyclic : Proved [7]


# Proof:

000. | BH = BD [C0] =(AR Deduction)> BD:BH = BH:BD [0]
001. | ∠(BD,BH) = ∠(BD,BH) [T0], BD:BH = BH:BD [0], ▲BDH has the same orientation as ▲BDH [N0] =(r63 SAS Similarity of triangles (Reverse))> ▲BDH ≅ ▲BHD [1]
002. | ▲BDH has the same orientation as ▲BDH [N0], ▲BDH ≅ ▲BHD [1] =(r53 Properties of similar triangles (Reverse))> ∠(BD,DH) = ∠(DH,BH) [2]
003. | AH ⟂ BC [C1], BC ⟂ DH [C2] =(AR Deduction)> DH ∥ AH [3]
004. | DH ∥ AH [3] =(r28 Overlapping parallels)> A, D, H are collinear [4]
005. | A, D, H are collinear [4], A ≠ D [N1], A ≠ H [N2], D ≠ H [N3] =(r82 Parallel from collinear)> AD ∥ AH [5]
006. | ∠(BD,DH) = ∠(DH,BH) [2], AD ∥ AH [5], AC ⟂ BH [C3], AH ⟂ BC [C1], BC ⟂ DH [C2] =(AR Deduction)> ∠(AC,AD) = ∠(BC,BD) [6]
007. | ∠(AC,AD) = ∠(BC,BD) [6], B, C, D are not collinear [N4] =(r04 Congruent angles are in a cyclic)> ABCD are cyclic [7]


# Appendix: AR deductions:



000. | Ratio Chasing to prove BD:BH = BH:BD:
| Premise BH = BD gives a linear equation with coefficients {BD²: 1, BH²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {BD²: 2, BH²: -2} that gives BD:BH = BH:BD



003. | Angle Chasing to prove DH ∥ AH:
| Premise AH ⟂ BC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AH: 1, BC: -1}
| Premise BC ⟂ DH gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC: 1, DH: -1}
| Then {AH: 1, BC: -1} + 1 x {BC: 1, DH: -1}
|    = {AH: 1, DH: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AH: 1, DH: -1} that gives DH ∥ AH



006. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(AC,AD) = ∠(BC,BD):
| Premise ∠(BD,DH) = ∠(DH,BH) gives a linear equation with coefficients {BD: -1, BH: -1, DH: 2}
| Premise AD ∥ AH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD: 1, AH: -1}
| Then {BD: -1, BH: -1, DH: 2} + 1 x {AD: 1, AH: -1}
|    = {BD: -1, BH: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AH: -1}
| Premise AC ⟂ BH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, BH: -1}
| Then {BD: -1, BH: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AH: -1} + -1 x {AC: 1, BH: -1}
|    = {BD: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AH: -1, AC: -1}
| Premise AH ⟂ BC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AH: 1, BC: -1}
| Then {BD: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AH: -1, AC: -1} + 1 x {AH: 1, BC: -1}
|    = {BD: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AC: -1, BC: -1}
| Premise BC ⟂ DH gives a linear equation with coefficients {BC: 1, DH: -1}
| Then {BD: -1, DH: 2, AD: 1, AC: -1, BC: -1} + 2 x {BC: 1, DH: -1}
|    = {BD: -1, AD: 1, AC: -1, BC: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {BD: -1, AD: 1, AC: -1, BC: 1} that gives ∠(AC,AD) = ∠(BC,BD)

End of proof.

